[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-50351"] :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta $, at $\\theta =\\pi $,  given that \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi } & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\pi )}{{x}^{′}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi }{(-\\frac{1}{\\pi })} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-348107/field-1/f5d50425dfe5d2ba78987ee35d09cbbc9948e504437c7c10e47855411f6bd91c" :answer/id #uuid "c264b50f-86d5-5e48-92b6-3862dab3d406" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sin(\\theta)+\\cos(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348107/field-1/aca3be2c24250988b8c9f30d63a6021b64097324867d5a3839b547046047e503" :answer/id #uuid "989fa439-7efd-53b4-9730-4439ff2296bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sin(\\theta)-\\cos(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-348107/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "248483c1-fdf8-5ddc-8876-5cf6ec75e8ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-348107/field-1/f5d50425dfe5d2ba78987ee35d09cbbc9948e504437c7c10e47855411f6bd91c" :answer-field/choices ["q-348107/field-1/f5d50425dfe5d2ba78987ee35d09cbbc9948e504437c7c10e47855411f6bd91c" "q-348107/field-1/aca3be2c24250988b8c9f30d63a6021b64097324867d5a3839b547046047e503"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-348107" :question/id #uuid "72209b1c-5622-5751-b8d7-9f75e7528fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-348107" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/assets/70c737e1fbbf63b3551763e48f4d7a956ffcc04ff68cced4f813b28e87630eb3.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-348107/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-348107"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353217/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "0c8a4686-f62d-54b8-a054-7eb5f82476e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353217/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7db9fe14-21dc-5c90-892c-51d6907295a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353217/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-353217/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353217" :question/id #uuid "6b82aa04-1308-5cb1-86b9-3f43159c9720" :question/math-academy-id "q-353217" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /8} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353217/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353217"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353229/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" :answer/id #uuid "6c3e61d8-da78-5518-8c45-66b2a356b7ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\cot(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353229/field-1/eb039d5c8c53c428103f2f47e214428c8b584c2b411f79d79a9cf5217e4fb542" :answer/id #uuid "9fb83254-9cec-548c-a7d6-f2cd1f62e321" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cot(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353229/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb8cddf-2d34-5e12-918a-e66ccaf50723" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353229/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" :answer-field/choices ["q-353229/field-1/af44a4a62fb996231c254b12e47704518673b7e39ba967ccef8467bccf1da7a4" "q-353229/field-1/eb039d5c8c53c428103f2f47e214428c8b584c2b411f79d79a9cf5217e4fb542"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353229" :question/id #uuid "e4817e4d-ca58-5b1e-9fce-e6c840ae3869" :question/math-academy-id "q-353229" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=22,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=22,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353229/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353229"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353245/field-1/26c95ee9a2f7b8df3ca98abf1b514496a31f72325e076ade3f9b95e83d19c5e1" :answer/id #uuid "3fcf877c-c106-565f-8c46-029783eab261" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin(2\\theta)+2\\cos(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353245/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f224d6d-c365-5555-b5db-deb3834a2b1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353245/field-1/26c95ee9a2f7b8df3ca98abf1b514496a31f72325e076ade3f9b95e83d19c5e1" :answer-field/choices ["q-353245/field-1/26c95ee9a2f7b8df3ca98abf1b514496a31f72325e076ade3f9b95e83d19c5e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353245" :question/id #uuid "e3fb7427-f267-5782-ab94-56397a75064d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353245" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/assets/7b11eccd4d3e5e1e90565219ce9b5098cd5aaeafe67c64d5e148245e17a6d355.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{((2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{((2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}{\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353245/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353245"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
